>> ddply(d, .(year), function(x) data.frame(mean_rating = mean(x$year)))
>   year mean_rating
> 1 2000        2000
> 2 2005        2005
> I'd like to use the syntax
>> ddply(d, .(year), newdf, mean_rating = mean(year))
> ... I wrote that newdf() myself to make the above work, like so:
> newdf = function(.data, ...) {
>   e = eval(substitute(list(...)), .data, parent.frame())
>   data.frame(e)
> }
> ... But, I suspect such a function might already exist, and in any case I'm
> new to R meta-ish programming and wrote that just by hacking up the
> transform() source.
> Does anyone whether I'm re-inventing the wheel?

I really like this idea.  I don't know of any functions that do this
already, and it would be a nice addition to plyr.

I think you can simplify the function a little:

newdf <- function(.data, ...) {
  eval(substitute(data.frame(...)), .data, parent.frame())
}

But I think it needs a better name ;)

Hadley

